      "summary": "This document is a synthetic Adverse Event Report Form generated for testing purposes. The patient is a 54-year-old female weighing 68 kg with a medical history of hypertension and type 2 diabetes. The reporter is Dr. Amara Osei, a physician based in the United Kingdom. The suspect product involved is Cardiozan, administered orally at a dose of 20 mg once daily starting on 03 March 2026. The patient experienced a widespread erythematous rash with pruritus starting on 11 March 2026. The outcome of the reaction is reported as recovered, and the event is classified as non-serious. A laboratory results table shows notable high values for white cell count at 11.8 x10^9/L, eosinophils at 0.9 x10^9/L, and C-reactive protein at 24 mg/L. Haemoglobin and platelet levels are also listed in the table and are flagged as normal. Several expected details are missing from this report. The document does not state the stop date of the suspect product or whether the therapy was discontinued. It is not stated if any corrective treatment was administered for the rash. The patient's initials, date of birth, and specific reporter contact details or institution are also not stated.",
